<ip addr>
Tests: Layer 1-2 (is the interface even alive?)
Shows your network interfaces and their assigned IP addresses.

What to look for: Is the interface UP? (state UP vs state DOWN)
Does it have an IP address assigned at all? (inet 192.168.x.x/24)
What's the subnet mask (the /24 part)? This tells you the size of your local network

If there's no IP address here, nothing else downstream will work — you haven't even gotten DHCP or manual config working yet.

<ip route>
Tests: Layer 3 (do you know how to leave your local network?)
Shows your routing table — most importantly, your default gateway.

What to look for: A line like default via 192.168.1.1 dev eth0 — this is your default gateway
Without this line, your machine only knows how to talk to devices on its own subnet. Anything outside → dead end

{ping <Ubuntu-IP>}
Tests: Layer 3 connectivity (can you actually reach the other machine?)

Sends ICMP echo requests and waits for replies.

What to look for: Replies coming back = basic IP connectivity works between the two VMs
Destination Host Unreachable = your machine doesn't know how to route there (often a gateway/subnet issue)
Request timed out = packet was sent but nothing came back (could be firewall dropping ICMP, or the host is down)
No output at all / immediate error = often means wrong IP or interface issue

Important: ping succeeding only proves Layer 3 works. It says nothing about whether any service (web server, SSH, etc.) is running on that machine.

<nslookup google.com and dig google.com>

Tests: DNS resolution (Application-layer name lookup)
Both do the same core job — resolve a domain name to an IP — but dig gives much more detail.

What to look for in nslookup: The Server line (which DNS server answered) — is it your expected resolver, or a fallback?
The Address returned — a real, valid-looking public IP

What to look for in dig:

The ANSWER SECTION — the actual A record returned
status: NOERROR = successful resolution; status: NXDOMAIN = the domain doesn't exist
Query time — how long resolution took
Which server it queried (SERVER: line at the bottom)

If this step fails but ping <Ubuntu-IP> worked, that tells you: your basic IP connectivity is fine, but DNS specifically is broken (wrong DNS server configured, DNS server unreachable, or upstream DNS issue) — a very common and important distinction to isolate.

nc -vz <Ubuntu-IP> 80 and nc -vz <Ubuntu-IP> 443

Tests: Layer 4, specific port/service availability

nc -vz (netcat, verbose, zero-I/O scan mode) checks whether a specific TCP port is open without sending real application data.

What to look for:

succeeded! / Connection ... succeeded = something is actively listening on that port and completed the TCP handshake
Connection refused = the host is reachable (Layer 3 is fine), but nothing is listening on that port — no service bound to it, or the service isn't running
Timeout / no response = could mean a firewall is silently dropping the traffic on that port (very different from "refused" — refused means "I got there but nobody's home," timeout often means "something blocked me on the way")

This is the step that distinguishes "the machine is up" (ping) from "the specific service is up" (nc).

curl -I http://<Ubuntu-IP>

Tests: Layer 7, does the actual HTTP service respond correctly?

-I fetches only the HTTP headers, not the full page body — fast way to check if a web server is answering properly.

What to look for:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK = web server is running and serving content normally
HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden = server's up, but access is denied (permissions/config issue)
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found = server's up, but nothing at that path
Connection refused = contradicts your nc test on port 80 — worth double-checking
Server: header — tells you what web server software is running (Apache, nginx, etc.)
Putting it together

This whole sequence is a top-down elimination process:

ip addr → do I have an address at all?
ip route → do I know how to leave my subnet?
ping → can I reach the target at the network layer?
nslookup/dig → does name resolution work (separate from raw IP reachability)?
nc -vz → is the specific port open (Transport layer)?
curl -I → is the actual service behaving correctly (Application layer)?
